                    <!-- Introduction section -->
                    <p class="lead text-xl text-primary font-medium mb-6">Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (TMS) therapy is widely recognized as a highly effective, remarkably safe, and exceptionally well-tolerated treatment for severe depression and various other mental health conditions.</p>
                    <p>Unlike systemic treatments such as traditional oral antidepressant medications, which circulate throughout the entire body and can cause a wide array of widespread side effects, TMS is a targeted, localized treatment.</p>
                    <p>This essential distinction means the side effects associated with TMS are typically minimal, temporary, and confined solely to the area of stimulation on the head.</p>

                    <h2 class="text-3xl text-primary mt-12 mb-5" style="font-family: 'Bauhaus Soft', cursive;">The Localized Nature of TMS: Why It Matters</h2>
                    <p>To truly understand the side effect profile of TMS, one must first understand how the technology works.</p>
                    <p>During a session, a specialized magnetic coil is placed gently against your scalp. It targets the area of the brain known to be underactive in individuals suffering from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-light">Major Depressive Disorder (MDD)</a>. Read more about this mechanism on our <a href="how-tms-works.php" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-light">How TMS Works page</a>.</p>
                    <p>Because the magnetic field only reaches a few centimeters into the skull, it stimulates the targeted brain tissue without systematically affecting the rest of your body's organs.</p>

